  "meta": {
    "title": "Third symbol from the end is 1 (the DFA)",
    "blurb": "The deterministic machine for a language an NFA says in four states. It must remember the last three symbols — all eight combinations — and no DFA with fewer states exists. The textbook case of the subset construction’s blow-up.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "100",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "0110",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "11111",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "0101",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "11",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "011",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "10001",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "subset-construction",
        "blow-up",
        "nfa-vs-dfa"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "chapter": "Hopcroft, Motwani & Ullman, §2.3.6",
      "readme": "Each state is named by the last three symbols read (the start pretends the word was preceded by 000). Reading *b* in state *xyz* moves to *yzb*; the states whose name begins with 1 accept.\n\n**Why eight is the minimum.** Take two different three-bit strings; they differ in some position. Append enough symbols to push that position to third-from-last, and one string is accepted while the other is not. So any DFA must keep all eight apart. For \"*n*th from the end\" the same argument gives 2ⁿ states — while the nondeterministic machine needs only *n* + 1, because it simply *guesses* when the third-from-last symbol goes by.\n\nCompare this entry with **Third symbol from the end is 1 (the NFA)**: the library fingerprints both to the same language."
    }
  }
}
